A||||10||sailordude||Taking my car off the Road for the Winter||||5||
Z||000000||sailordude||11-26-2001||02:05 PM||||Hey guys,<BR>   This is the first year that I've owned my fiero, and the previous owners never let the car see snow, so I figured why should I let it.  So my question to you all is what do I need to do to prep my car for storage for the winter?  Also what do I need to do during the winter to keep my car healthy.  I've asked a few people around town here, but I haven't found any definate answers to what I should do.  I guess my main worries are animals moving into my exaust for a winter home, and also moisture in my gas tank.  Is there anything else that I should be worried about?  Should I leave it with a full, or empty tank of gas.  Should I stuff something (a rag or something) in the exaust tips.  Disconnect the battery or leave it hooked up?  Anything else that you all think I should do please let me know.<P>Thanks again guys,<P>Darren||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||11-26-2001||04:42 PM||||I've stored my 87 GT outside in the winter since 1988. I've never had rodents take up residence in the exhaust system, but if that is a concern, cover them.  Just remember to remove it in the spring. Fill the tank add some "Stabil" to the tank.  I'd recommend disconnecting the battery, mine died last winter, first one to do that in 13 years.  You may want to put the car up on stands (I don't)to keep a flat spot from developing on the tires.  Find a good car cover (usually a pricy item) Cover the car.   I've put a tarp over the cover, and anchored to the wheels with bungee cords. This is to keep the tarp and cover from blowing off during the wonderful winter weather storms.  Make sure the metal clip on the bungee cord doesn't meet the body, or during the flex of the wind various gouges appear on the car's body by the spring.||||reg||1
Z||000002||REDHOTT88||11-26-2001||05:17 PM||||This is the first year I'll be storing my Fiero too. Just got a cover from the Fiero store. 160.00 OUCH! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>But it just rained last night and cleared this morning. I checked under the cover and the car was wet! Is this normal?<P>I ordered the OUTSIDE cover too&gt;&gt;&gt; <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR>1988 Formula 2.8L <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (red & shiny all over!)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mickey_Moose||11-26-2001||05:58 PM||||I have yet to see a cover made of fabric keep the water out...best to cover the car with the soft cover...then use a tarp made of the plastic type material. Secure it down good to keep the wind from walking off with it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Tim<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||REDHOTT88||11-26-2001||06:23 PM||||<BR>Sounds good... I'll try it <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Thanks||||reg||10
Z||000005||2ndchancefiero||11-26-2001||08:14 PM|||| This is my first year with my fiero too and I have to store it outside this winter. I Placed a car cover on it and then a tarp over that. I think just a tarp without the cover may scratch the paint, plus it does'nt breath and if condensation gets under it you'll have big paint problems. Also i read on a sight warm the car up and before it cools place heavy duty plastic over the exhaust outlets to keeps condenstaion out (as well as rodents) so it does'nt rust out. Of course i added fuel stabalizer too. Hope this helps..Lou<P>------------------<BR>1987 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||sailordude||11-26-2001||08:23 PM||||I'm keeping my car in a garage.  So you guys think I still need a car cover?||||reg||1
Z||000007||REDHOTT88||11-26-2001||08:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sailordude:<BR><B>I'm keeping my car in a garage.  So you guys think I still need a car <P>cover?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>  DUST!!!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR>1988 Formula 2.8L <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (red & shiny all over!)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||LS1swap||11-26-2001||09:56 PM||||I store a couple of cars each winter personally I like to start them up about once or twice a month. If you plan on not running it all winter you should spay fogging oil in each cylinder. In either case fill the tank before storage for you will get far less condensation in a full tank than an empty one. Stable is a good idea as well.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shinerdc||11-26-2001||11:31 PM||||i have an 84 sitting on blocks right now....sunroof open an inch plus for venting...i use a plastic tablecloth with a thermal backing...nice and soft....then i cover that/the car with a tents fly...works great...just lucky i had a couple of spare fly's from my scout troop....knew those things would come in handy...i'm keeping the cost of a cover in my pocket and using it for a new head when i find it...good luck..<BR>Shinerdc||||reg||1
Z||000010||California Kid||11-27-2001||01:00 AM||||Just a word of caution to those not using a "breathable" car cover. "solid" covers will create a corrosion chamber around your whole car by trapping humidity! It has to breath to let moisture out, that comes from underneath the car.<P>If you are storing in the garage, make sure you have at least one roof vent so that the garage can breath on a continuous basis, it will be better for everything metal in your garage. If your heated (non vented), fabricate an adjustable damper.||||reg||4
